//! The export table: the closed set of files Frame Job can write, and
//! nothing whatever about writing one of them (spec §7.8).
//!
//! Split out of `export.rs` under the size rule (core/CLAUDE.md: a module
//! at ~600 lines gives something up BEFORE it takes anything on), which
//! that file paid once already — `describe` and the override readers went
//! to `export_overrides.rs` in road map 2's phase 5, and this is the
//! second cut, taken in phase 7 for the same reason and along a different
//! seam. The rule both follow: cut where somebody can NAME the line, and
//! re-export the piece taken out whole, so not one path anywhere else
//! changes.
//!
//! # The seam: a fact about the world, not about a document
//!
//! Everything in this file is a constant, and none of it can be
//! influenced by a project. [`Container`] is the set of files this
//! application knows how to produce and the four questions each one
//! answers about ITSELF — its extension, its UTI, whether a leading index
//! is worth rewriting, whether it can hold sound at all. [`Preset`] is one
//! encoding placed in one of those containers, and [`PRESETS`] is every
//! such row in menu order. An APNG's type identifier is PNG's whatever
//! the timeline holds; GIF caps its size because of what a palettised
//! frame-by-frame format costs, not because of anybody's canvas.
//!
//! `export.rs` keeps the other half, and the other half is the one with a
//! `&self`: `Project::export_settings` is the only code that reads a
//! canvas, a duration, a rate or an override, and it is where those meet
//! a row from here. So the line runs between them in one direction only —
//! the resolver knows the table, the table knows nothing about a project
//! — which is also why the module doc that explains the whole export
//! design stays over there rather than following the rows here. This file
//! says what CAN be written. That one says what this particular document,
//! written that way, comes out as.
//!
//! # Paths
//!
//! `export.rs` re-exports [`presets`] and [`preset`] at their old names,
//! so `crate::export::presets()` (`ffi_query.rs`) and
//! `crate::export::preset(id)` (`export_size.rs`) resolve exactly as they
//! did — the same trick, and for the same reason, as the `describe`
//! re-export sitting a few lines above them. What the cut did cost is
//! visibility, mechanically: the [`Preset`] fields the resolver reads and
//! the four [`Container`] questions it asks are `pub(crate)` now, where
//! one file had been able to keep them private. [`fps_json`] came across
//! with the table rather than staying with the resolver and is
//! `pub(crate)` for it: both sides spell a rate, and its being one
//! function is the whole of why they cannot disagree about the shape.

use serde_json::Value;

pub(crate) use crate::export_container::Container;
pub(crate) use crate::export_encoder::{EncoderSpeed, Writer};
pub(crate) use crate::export_preset::{fps_json, Preset};
use crate::time::Fps;

/// The §7.8 set: one row per ENCODING, in menu order. A row is a container
/// and a codec and nothing else — no size, no rate — so an export is at the
/// document's own size and rate unless a person asks for another, through
/// the Size popup the sheet fills from `Project::export_sizes` or the
/// overrides a script sends. The size used to be part of the row ("H.264
/// 1080p", "HEVC 4K"), and that shape made the format menu two questions
/// asked as one: picking HEVC meant picking 1080p or 4K with it, and a
/// 1440p or a vertical document had no row that fit it at all. Two
/// questions get two controls, and the format is only ever the encoding.
///
/// The two image rows are the exception and keep a size and a rate,
/// because for them the numbers ARE what makes the row worth having: a GIF
/// or an APNG costs frames × pixels with no inter-frame prediction to
/// spend against, so "at the document's size and rate" is a file no one
/// can send, and the cap is the point of the format rather than a default
/// it happens to carry (§7.8; the row comments argue each number). Both
/// caps are still defaults a size or fps override replaces, and
/// `export_sizes` lists each as the first row of its list.
///
/// The FIRST row is the default: `rc_export_presets` is "in menu order"
/// and the export sheet opens on its first entry, so whatever sits here is
/// what ⌘E writes unless a person picks otherwise. H.264 in MP4 is the
/// file every player and every upload form takes, which is why it leads
/// and HEVC — half the size, not yet everywhere — follows.
///
/// Each movie row carries an `AVAssetExportSession` preset name for the
/// record: the three "highest quality" names are the ones a session would
/// take the composition's own size under rather than fit it to a fixed
/// frame, which is exactly a row with no size of its own. Nothing runs a
/// session any more (§7.8), so on the Swift side the name is only the
/// signal that AVFoundation can write the row at all; the two image rows
/// carry none, which is the signal to write them frame by frame through
/// ImageIO instead. The two LPCM rows repeat `audio_m4a`'s name for the
/// same reason and with the field doc's caveat: AVFoundation has no
/// session preset for LPCM, and a null would route a WAV to ImageIO.
///
/// **No row carries a default bitrate, deliberately.** On the session path
/// the preset name IS the ladder — `AVAssetExportSession` exposes no way to
/// set a rate — so a number here would be one the export cannot honour,
/// which is worse than none at all. On the writer path VideoToolbox derives
/// a rate from the frame size, which beats any constant this table could
/// hold: one that suits 4K wastes half the file at 720p. ProRes is fixed by
/// its profile and ignores a request outright, and GIF and APNG have no
/// bitrate to set. A bitrate is therefore something the user asks for — the
/// `video_bitrate` / `audio_bitrate` overrides — never something a preset
/// assumes on their behalf.
const PRESETS: [Preset; 15] = [
    // The default (see above). The ids are the `export` tool's vocabulary
    // and every script that calls it spells them, so a row is named for
    // the encoding alone — `h264`, not `h264_document` — because the size
    // is no longer anything the id could say.
    Preset {
        id: "h264",
        name: "H.264 (MP4)",
        container: Container::Mp4,
        codec: "h264",
        video: true,
        alpha: false,
        size: None,
        fps: None,
        avf_preset: Some("AVAssetExportPresetHighestQuality"),
        writer: None,
    },
    Preset {
        id: "hevc",
        name: "HEVC (MP4)",
        container: Container::Mp4,
        codec: "hevc",
        video: true,
        alpha: false,
        size: None,
        fps: None,
        avf_preset: Some("AVAssetExportPresetHEVCHighestQuality"),
        writer: None,
    },
    // HEVC with an alpha channel (spec §11 G-33), directly after the row it
    // is a variant of. It is the DELIVERY row that keeps transparency — a
    // title or a lower third a web page or another editor plays over its
    // own background, at HEVC's size where `prores_4444` is a master — and
    // it is a MOV because that is the container AVFoundation writes the
    // codec into (it refuses it an `.mp4`). The codec is spelled as the id
    // is, `hevc_alpha`: `ExporterWriter.codecType(for:)` maps it to
    // `AVVideoCodecType.hevcWithAlpha`, and the sheet's Quality tiers and
    // bit-rate controls read the `hevc` prefix, so it takes HEVC's.
    Preset {
        id: "hevc_alpha",
        name: "HEVC with Alpha (MOV)",
        container: Container::Mov,
        codec: "hevc_alpha",
        video: true,
        alpha: true,
        size: None,
        fps: None,
        avf_preset: Some("AVAssetExportPresetHEVCHighestQualityWithAlpha"),
        writer: None,
    },
    // HEVC Main 10 (spec §11 PR-3): `hevc`'s MP4 from the compositor's
    // 16-bit frames, so a 10-bit source survives to a 10-bit delivery file.
    // After `hevc_alpha`, which G-33 keeps directly after `hevc`. The codec
    // spelling is the id's: `ExporterWriter.codecType(for:)` maps it to
    // `.hevc` with the Main 10 profile, and the `hevc` prefix gives it
    // HEVC's Quality tiers.
    Preset {
        id: "hevc_10bit",
        name: "HEVC 10-bit (MP4)",
        container: Container::Mp4,
        codec: "hevc_10bit",
        video: true,
        alpha: false,
        size: None,
        fps: None,
        avf_preset: Some("AVAssetExportPresetHEVCHighestQuality"),
        writer: None,
    },
    // AV1 in MP4 (spec §11 CX-1, CX-12, CX-14), 10-bit, an HDR row through
    // `HDR_ROWS`. SVT-AV1 encodes (`writer`); AVFoundation still writes the
    // container, which is what the passthrough name records, so the row is
    // even-sized and routed as every AVFoundation row is.
    Preset {
        id: "av1",
        name: "AV1 (MP4)",
        container: Container::Mp4,
        codec: "av1",
        video: true,
        alpha: false,
        size: None,
        fps: None,
        avf_preset: Some("AVAssetExportPresetPassthrough"),
        writer: Some(Writer::SvtAv1),
    },
    // AV1 in WebM (spec §11 CX-15): `av1`'s encoding — the same encoder,
    // speeds, cap and HDR — in a WebM the core writes (`webm_writer.rs`),
    // with Opus sound. The passthrough name records that the composition is
    // read back through AVFoundation, so the row is even-sized and routed as
    // every AVFoundation row is until `Exporter.pipeline` reads the container.
    Preset {
        id: "av1_webm",
        name: "AV1 (WebM)",
        container: Container::Webm,
        codec: "av1",
        video: true,
        alpha: false,
        size: None,
        fps: None,
        avf_preset: Some("AVAssetExportPresetPassthrough"),
        writer: Some(Writer::SvtAv1),
    },
    // VP9 in WebM (spec §11 CX-16): libvpx at its one speed (CX-3) into the
    // same core-written WebM with Opus; Profile 0 8-bit for an SDR picture,
    // Profile 2 10-bit for HLG and PQ, so an HDR row.
    Preset {
        id: "vp9_webm",
        name: "VP9 (WebM)",
        container: Container::Webm,
        codec: "vp9",
        video: true,
        alpha: false,
        size: None,
        fps: None,
        avf_preset: Some("AVAssetExportPresetPassthrough"),
        writer: Some(Writer::Libvpx),
    },
    // VP9 with alpha in WebM (spec §11 CX-17): `vp9_webm`'s 8-bit Profile 0
    // picture and a second libvpx stream of its alpha, written as each video
    // block's BlockAdditional 1 — the format Chrome and Firefox play with
    // transparency. One of the four rows with a fourth channel, so the shape
    // rule binds it; 8-bit SDR only, so not an HDR row: an HLG project writes
    // the SDR version.
    Preset {
        id: "vp9_alpha_webm",
        name: "VP9 with Alpha (WebM)",
        container: Container::Webm,
        codec: "vp9",
        video: true,
        alpha: true,
        size: None,
        fps: None,
        avf_preset: Some("AVAssetExportPresetPassthrough"),
        writer: Some(Writer::Libvpx),
    },
    Preset {
        id: "prores_422",
        name: "ProRes 422 (MOV)",
        container: Container::Mov,
        codec: "prores422",
        video: true,
        alpha: false,
        size: None,
        fps: None,
        avf_preset: Some("AVAssetExportPresetAppleProRes422LPCM"),
        writer: None,
    },
    // The MASTERING row that keeps transparency (road map 2, phase 7) —
    // one of the four rows with a fourth channel and not the only one:
    // `hevc_alpha` and `vp9_alpha_webm` above and `apng` below carry one too
    // and say so at their own rows. The four are not redundant, which is why
    // no row's comment may be written as if it were alone: this is a MASTER,
    // in a container AVFoundation writes, whose next reader is another
    // application; `hevc_alpha` is the delivery movie and `vp9_alpha_webm`
    // the browser's; and `apng` is a frame sequence with a null `avf_preset`
    // and no audio track at all. `alpha`'s field doc holds the rule all four
    // answer to, and `core/tests/export.rs` pins the four by name.
    //
    // ProRes 4444 is a master or an intermediate: a file whose next
    // reader is another application rather than a viewer — a lower third
    // or a title card cut over somebody else's footage, a screencast going
    // back into a compositor, a graphic handed to a motion designer. The
    // fourth channel is the whole of why it earns a row beside
    // `prores_422`, which shares its container, its colour and its
    // editing-master posture and differs in exactly this: 422 cannot carry
    // alpha at any bitrate. Nor can the `h264` or `hevc` row stand in —
    // neither has an alpha channel in the profile `ExporterWriter` writes
    // for it, so an export of a transparent canvas through one of them is
    // a picture flattened onto black with no way back. `hevc_alpha` does
    // keep the channel, as a lossy 8-bit 4:2:0 delivery file, which is not
    // what a master is.
    //
    // It sits AFTER 422 rather than before it because a row's position is
    // an invitation: most exports of a finished timeline have nothing
    // under them and want the smaller of the two files, so 422 stays the
    // ProRes people reach for first and 4444 is the one you go looking for
    // when you need the channel.
    Preset {
        id: "prores_4444",
        name: "ProRes 4444 (MOV)",
        container: Container::Mov,
        // The spelling `ExporterWriter.codecType(for:)` already maps to
        // `AVVideoCodecType.proRes4444`; it accepts `prores_4444` too, and
        // this one is chosen for the same reason `prores422` is — the
        // codec field is the codec's own name, where the underscore-and-id
        // spelling is the PRESET's.
        codec: "prores4444",
        video: true,
        alpha: true,
        size: None,
        fps: None,
        // Non-null, and not optional: a null `avf_preset` is the ONE
        // routing signal to the ImageIO writer (`avf_preset`'s field doc),
        // which would meet a `"mov"` container it has never heard of and
        // refuse a format this table advertises. The name is
        // AVFoundation's real ProRes 4444 preset and is a record of what
        // writes the row, nothing more — nothing has run a session since
        // M2.
        avf_preset: Some("AVAssetExportPresetAppleProRes4444LPCM"),
        writer: None,
    },
    Preset {
        id: "audio_m4a",
        name: "Audio only (M4A)",
        container: Container::M4a,
        codec: "aac",
        video: false,
        alpha: false,
        size: None,
        fps: None,
        avf_preset: Some("AVAssetExportPresetAppleM4A"),
        writer: None,
    },
    // The two uncompressed audio rows (road map 2, phase 5). M4A is what
    // you send; these are what you hand to someone else's tool — a mix
    // going out to a mastering engineer, a bed going into a podcast
    // editor, a stem a DAW will re-encode anyway — where a second lossy
    // generation is the one thing you cannot take back.
    //
    // The codec is `lpcm`, not a container spelling, because the container
    // and the codec are separate questions here and `ExportWriter` has to
    // answer the codec one to pick its `AVAssetWriterInput` settings: the
    // mix is read as float and quantised once by the export's master
    // (spec §11 AU-27) to interleaved 48 kHz LPCM, so a WAV is those
    // samples little-endian and an AIFF the same samples big-endian, which
    // the CONTAINER decides.
    //
    // 16-bit by default, and 24 an override rather than a row: the
    // `audio_bits` request beside the bitrates (AU-29), offered on these
    // two rows only (`Preset::takes_audio_bits`).
    Preset {
        id: "audio_wav",
        name: "Audio only (WAV)",
        container: Container::Wav,
        codec: "lpcm",
        video: false,
        alpha: false,
        size: None,
        fps: None,
        // Not a lie the resolver tells: see `avf_preset`'s field doc. The
        // name says "AVFoundation writes this row", and nothing more.
        avf_preset: Some("AVAssetExportPresetAppleM4A"),
        writer: None,
    },
    Preset {
        id: "audio_aiff",
        name: "Audio only (AIFF)",
        container: Container::Aiff,
        codec: "lpcm",
        video: false,
        alpha: false,
        size: None,
        fps: None,
        avf_preset: Some("AVAssetExportPresetAppleM4A"),
        writer: None,
    },
    Preset {
        id: "gif",
        name: "Animated GIF",
        container: Container::Gif,
        codec: "gif",
        video: true,
        alpha: false,
        // 480 × 270 at 12.5 fps, and both numbers are the point of the
        // preset rather than an afterthought. GIF stores every frame as a
        // whole picture over a 256-colour palette with no inter-frame
        // prediction, so its size is frames × pixels and nothing else:
        // the same ten seconds carries sixteen times the pixels at 1080p
        // and a hundred and thirty at 4K, which is why a full-rate 4K GIF
        // is a file no one can send. At 480 × 270 a ten-second clip lands
        // in single-digit megabytes, and 270 keeps the 16:9 shape the
        // other fixed-size rows use.
        //
        // 12.5 fps is not a rounding of 12. A GIF frame delay is stored in
        // hundredths of a second, so only rates that divide 100 exactly
        // survive the write, and 12.5 (8 cs) is the lowest of those that
        // still reads as motion when a cursor crosses a screen recording.
        // 10 fps (10 cs) visibly steps; 15 fps would want 6.67 cs, which
        // viewers round to 7 and then play at 14.3.
        size: Some((480, 270)),
        fps: Some(Fps { num: 25, den: 2 }),
        avf_preset: None,
        writer: None,
    },
    Preset {
        id: "apng",
        name: "Animated PNG",
        container: Container::Apng,
        codec: "apng",
        video: true,
        // One of the four rows that keep a transparent canvas
        // transparent, and the only one that is not a movie — a lossless
        // frame sequence any browser shows, with no sound.
        alpha: true,
        // 960 × 540 at 25 fps. APNG is true colour and lossless, so it
        // costs more per pixel than GIF but has no palette to quantise to:
        // twice the linear size buys back the detail GIF spends on its 256
        // colours, and the format is worth choosing only for that.
        // The rate is free of GIF's constraint — an `fcTL` delay is an
        // exact rational, not a centisecond count — so it is a familiar 25
        // rather than an arithmetic one; that is half the 50 or 60 a screen
        // recording arrives at and smooth enough that the cap is invisible.
        size: Some((960, 540)),
        fps: Some(Fps { num: 25, den: 1 }),
        avf_preset: None,
        writer: None,
    },
];

/// Every preset, in menu order, as the JSON array `rc_export_presets`
/// returns.
pub fn presets() -> Value {
    Value::Array(PRESETS.iter().map(Preset::to_json).collect())
}

/// The row for `id`, or the error naming it. The ONE lookup: both
/// [`Project::export_settings`] and `Project::export_sizes` go through it,
/// so an unknown preset reads the same whichever of the two the sheet or
/// the `export` tool asked first, and a row cannot have sizes and no
/// settings or the other way round.
pub(crate) fn preset(id: &str) -> Result<&'static Preset, String> {
    PRESETS
        .iter()
        .find(|p| p.id == id)
        .ok_or_else(|| format!("unknown export preset \"{id}\""))
}

//! Export presets and the resolution of one into concrete settings
//! (spec §7.8).
//!
//! Rust decides, AVFoundation does: nothing here encodes anything. The job
//! is to turn "H.264, 1920 × 1080, the in/out range, 30 fps" into the exact
//! set of values Swift hands to the writer — the preset name, the output
//! file type, the time range, the frame size — so that the choice of format
//! is testable without a GPU and the MCP agent's `export` tool takes the
//! same vocabulary the UI does.
//!
//! A preset is an ENCODING — a container and a codec — and carries no size:
//! an export is at the document's own size unless one is asked for, and
//! the sizes on offer for a document and a format are a separate list,
//! [`Project::export_sizes`] in `export_size.rs`, whose rows reach the
//! resolver here as the same `width`/`height` overrides a script sends.
//!
//! Two of the presets name a format AVFoundation cannot write at all: an
//! animated GIF and an animated PNG are assembled frame by frame through
//! ImageIO. That changes nothing here except one field — those rows carry
//! no `avf_preset`, and a null `avf_preset` is Swift's signal to take the
//! image-sequence path instead of building a session. Everything else
//! (the size, the rate, the range, the file's UTI) is resolved identically,
//! because it means the same thing on either path.
//!
//! Four rows, `av1` (spec §11 CX-12), `av1_webm` (CX-15), `vp9_webm`
//! (CX-16) and `vp9_alpha_webm` (CX-17), are encoded by a bundled library
//! and not by AVFoundation, which still writes `av1`'s container (the core
//! writes the three WebMs): their `writer` names the route, and on the two
//! AV1 rows `encoder_speed` names SVT-AV1's speed; no other row's settings
//! carry either key, and the two VP9 rows' carry no speed, libvpx having one
//! (CX-3).
//!
//! The two uncompressed audio rows, `audio_wav` and `audio_aiff`, carry
//! `audio_bits`, 16 or 24 (spec §11 AU-29): 16 when not asked, and refused
//! by name on every other row.
//!
//! The three audio rows take `stems`, `tracks` or `names` (spec §11
//! AU-108): asked, the settings end with `stems`, the files beside the mix
//! resolved to `[{name, tracks}]` by `export_stems.rs`; a movie row refuses.
//!
//! The preset table is the single source of truth: [`presets`] serializes
//! it for the picker and [`Project::export_settings`] resolves against the
//! same rows, so a preset cannot exist in the menu and not in the resolver.
//!
//! How an override value is READ and REFUSED is `export_overrides.rs`,
//! and the rows themselves — the containers, and the presets written into
//! them — are `export_table.rs`. The vocabulary and the table: this file
//! is the design they serve and the resolution that brings the two of
//! them together against one project.

use serde_json::{Map, Value};

use crate::export_overrides::{
    audio_bits as audio_bits_override, boolean, dynamic_range, encoder_speed, fps as fps_override,
    loudness, object, positive_u32, range as range_override, stems as stems_override,
};
use crate::export_table::{fps_json, EncoderSpeed};
use crate::model::Project;
use crate::time;

/// Re-exported at its old path. Five modules outside this one
/// (`edit.rs`, `edit_props.rs`, `edit_action.rs`, `edit_paste.rs`,
/// `edit_track.rs`) name `crate::export::describe` for their own error
/// messages — it was never really an export function — so the phase-5 cut
/// that moved it into `export_overrides.rs` costs them nothing.
pub(crate) use crate::export_overrides::describe;

/// Re-exported at their old paths too, on the same terms and for the same
/// reason: the rows went out to `export_table.rs` under the size rule, and
/// the two callers that ask the table a question should not have to learn
/// which file the answer moved to. `ffi_query.rs` answers
/// `rc_export_presets` with `export::presets()`, and `export_size.rs`
/// looks a row up with `export::preset(id)` — the ONE lookup, which the
/// resolver below shares with it.
pub(crate) use crate::export_table::preset;
pub use crate::export_table::presets;

// ---- resolution ----------------------------------------------------------

impl Project {
    /// Resolves `preset_id` plus optional `overrides` against this project
    /// into the concrete settings Swift configures the export session with
    /// (`rc_export_settings`).
    ///
    /// Overrides may set `width`, `height`, `fps` (`[num, den]`), `range`
    /// (`{"start", "duration"}` in ticks), `optimize_for_network`, the two
    /// bitrates `video_bitrate` / `audio_bitrate` (bits per second,
    /// strictly positive), the two loudness figures `loudness_lufs` /
    /// `peak_dbtp`, `dynamic_range` (`hlg`, `pq` or `sdr`, spec §11
    /// HD-14), `encoder_speed` (`faster`, `balanced` or `smaller`, on a
    /// row whose bundled encoder has speeds, CX-12, CX-16) and `audio_bits`
    /// (16 or 24, on `audio_wav` and `audio_aiff`, AU-29) and `stems`
    /// (`tracks` or `names`, on the three audio rows, resolved to
    /// `[{name, tracks}]` last in the settings, AU-108); anything else in the object is ignored, so a newer UI
    /// can send fields this build does not know. Each is read and refused
    /// by `export_overrides.rs`, which is where the spelling of every one
    /// of them lives.
    ///
    /// A bitrate is emitted only when it was asked for. There is no default
    /// to fall back on (see `export_table::PRESETS`), and an absent key says
    /// exactly that — "let the encoder pick" — where a zero or a null would
    /// have to be re-interpreted as the same thing on the Swift side. The two
    /// are accepted for every preset, including the ones that cannot use them,
    /// on the same principle that reports a width for the audio-only row:
    /// one rule for reading overrides, and the format flags say what the
    /// output can actually carry.
    ///
    /// The two loudness figures follow the bitrates exactly — emitted only
    /// when asked for, accepted by every preset, either one alone: the
    /// ceiling is the export's master limiter's (spec §11 AU-27), which runs
    /// with or without a target.
    ///
    /// The range is snapped to the export's frame grid and clamped to the
    /// project, because an `AVAssetExportSession` `timeRange` that begins
    /// between two frames starts the export on a frame the user did not
    /// choose. A range that resolves to zero length is refused: an empty
    /// export is always a mistake, and AVFoundation reports it far later
    /// and far less clearly.
    ///
    /// `Err` for an unknown preset or a malformed override. This is a QUERY
    /// and not an edit: there is no "changes nothing" case, so it has no
    /// identity arm and no refusal arm (spec §5.5).
    pub fn export_settings(
        &self,
        preset_id: &str,
        overrides: Option<&Value>,
    ) -> Result<Value, String> {
        let preset = preset(preset_id)?;
        let overrides = object(overrides)?;
        // The picture the row writes (spec §11 HD-14 (c), `dynamic_range.rs`):
        // read before anything else, so a mistyped value is refused by name
        // whatever the row, and resolved against the canvas and the row.
        let picture = self.deliverable(preset, dynamic_range(overrides)?)?;
        // The bundled encoder's speed (spec §11 CX-12 (2)): read on any row
        // so a misspelling is named, then refused on a row it cannot apply
        // to, and Balanced when a writer row was not asked.
        let speed = match (encoder_speed(overrides)?, preset.takes_encoder_speed()) {
            (Some(_), false) => {
                return Err(format!(
                    "{} has no encoder speed; encoder_speed applies to av1 and av1_webm",
                    preset.id
                ))
            }
            (asked, true) => Some(asked.unwrap_or(EncoderSpeed::Balanced)),
            (None, false) => None,
        };
        // The uncompressed rows' bit depth (spec §11 AU-29), on the speed's
        // terms: read on any row so a 20 is named, refused on a row that
        // has no depth to choose, and 16 on those rows when not asked.
        let bits = match (audio_bits_override(overrides)?, preset.takes_audio_bits()) {
            (Some(_), false) => {
                return Err(format!(
                    "{} has no bit depth; audio_bits applies to audio_wav and audio_aiff",
                    preset.id
                ))
            }
            (asked, true) => Some(asked.unwrap_or(16)),
            (None, false) => None,
        };
        // The stems (spec §11 AU-108 (a)), on the depth's terms: read on any
        // row so a misspelling is named, refused on a movie row, and
        // resolved to the files they are only when asked.
        let stems = match (stems_override(overrides)?, preset.takes_stems()) {
            (Some(_), false) => {
                return Err(format!(
                    "{} has no stems; stems applies to audio_m4a, audio_wav and audio_aiff",
                    preset.id
                ))
            }
            (Some(mode), true) => Some(self.export_stems(mode)?),
            (None, _) => None,
        };

        // Size: the preset's cap if it has one, else the canvas — a movie
        // preset renders at whatever the project composites at, and a size
        // the sheet's popup or a script chose arrives as the override.
        let (preset_width, preset_height) = preset
            .size
            .unwrap_or((self.canvas.width, self.canvas.height));
        let width = positive_u32(overrides, "width")?.unwrap_or(preset_width);
        let height = positive_u32(overrides, "height")?.unwrap_or(preset_height);
        // What is reported is the size the FILE will have, so that the
        // sheet's readout, its Size popup and the `export` tool's result
        // all say the numbers the writer draws (`export_size.rs` has both
        // rules and their reasons). An image format is written with the
        // picture FITTED inside the size, never letterboxed and never
        // upscaled, so its numbers are the fit and not the box. A row that
        // needs even dimensions writes an odd edge one pixel smaller
        // (spec §11 G-33), whether the edge came from the canvas or was
        // asked for. Every other row writes the pair as it stands.
        let (width, height) = if preset.fits_inside_size() {
            crate::export_size::fit_within((self.canvas.width, self.canvas.height), (width, height))
        } else if preset.even_sized() {
            crate::export_size::even_size((width, height))
        } else {
            (width, height)
        };

        // The preset's rate, else the canvas — the image-sequence presets
        // cap the rate for the same reason they cap the size, and every
        // movie preset exports at the rate the project was cut at.
        let fps = fps_override(overrides, preset.fps.unwrap_or(self.canvas.fps))?;

        let project_duration = self.duration();
        let (mut start, mut duration) = range_override(overrides, (0, project_duration))?;
        // Snap first, then clamp: snapping can only move a bound by less
        // than a frame, so clamping afterwards cannot be undone by it.
        start = time::snap_to_frame(start, fps).clamp(0, project_duration);
        duration =
            time::snap_to_frame(duration, fps).clamp(0, project_duration.saturating_sub(start));
        if duration <= 0 {
            return Err(format!(
                "export range is empty: {duration} ticks at {start} in a project {project_duration} ticks long"
            ));
        }

        // Bits per second, or nothing at all. No preset supplies one, so an
        // absent key here reaches Swift as an absent key there and means
        // "the encoder's own ladder".
        let video_bitrate = positive_u32(overrides, "video_bitrate")?;
        let audio_bitrate = positive_u32(overrides, "audio_bitrate")?;

        // The integrated loudness target and the master limiter's true-peak
        // ceiling, on the bitrates' terms: emitted only when asked for, so
        // an absent `loudness_lufs` reaches Swift as an absent key and means
        // "do not normalise", and an absent `peak_dbtp` the master's default
        // (spec §11 AU-27). The reader is the one place their bounds live.
        let (loudness_lufs, peak_dbtp) = loudness(overrides)?;

        let optimize = boolean(
            overrides,
            "optimize_for_network",
            preset.container.optimizes_for_network(),
        )?;

        let mut range = Map::new();
        range.insert("start".to_string(), Value::from(start));
        range.insert("duration".to_string(), Value::from(duration));

        let mut out = Map::new();
        out.insert("preset".to_string(), Value::from(preset.id));
        // Null here means "AVFoundation cannot write this" — the GIF and
        // APNG rows — and is Swift's cue to take the ImageIO path. It is
        // NOT "pick a session preset yourself".
        out.insert(
            "avf_preset".to_string(),
            match preset.avf_preset {
                Some(name) => Value::from(name),
                None => Value::Null,
            },
        );
        out.insert(
            "container".to_string(),
            Value::from(preset.container.as_str()),
        );
        out.insert(
            "file_type".to_string(),
            Value::from(preset.container.file_type()),
        );
        out.insert("codec".to_string(), Value::from(preset.codec));
        // Width and height are reported even for the audio-only preset —
        // they cost nothing and let the sheet show one summary line for
        // every preset — but `video: false` is what Swift keys off.
        out.insert("width".to_string(), Value::from(width));
        out.insert("height".to_string(), Value::from(height));
        out.insert("fps".to_string(), fps_json(Some(fps)));
        if let Some(bps) = video_bitrate {
            out.insert("video_bitrate".to_string(), Value::from(bps));
        }
        if let Some(bps) = audio_bitrate {
            out.insert("audio_bitrate".to_string(), Value::from(bps));
        }
        if let Some(lufs) = loudness_lufs {
            out.insert("loudness_lufs".to_string(), Value::from(lufs));
        }
        if let Some(dbtp) = peak_dbtp {
            out.insert("peak_dbtp".to_string(), Value::from(dbtp));
        }
        out.insert("range".to_string(), Value::Object(range));
        // Audio is offered whenever the container can hold it: a project
        // with no sound simply produces no audio track, which is cheaper
        // than deciding here. A GIF or an APNG holds none at all.
        out.insert(
            "audio".to_string(),
            Value::from(preset.container.carries_audio()),
        );
        out.insert("video".to_string(), Value::from(preset.video));
        // Whether the ENCODING carries a fourth channel — true for
        // `prores_4444`, `hevc_alpha` and `apng`, and for nothing else the
        // table offers. `export_table.rs`'s `alpha` field doc is where
        // `apng` against `gif` is argued — a GIF shows a transparent pixel
        // and still answers false, because it has one palette index and no
        // fourth channel — and `core/tests/export.rs` pins the three rows
        // by name.
        //
        // It reads `preset.alpha` and not `self.canvas.background`, which
        // is the whole decision and is worth one sentence here as well as
        // in the field doc: this object is flat and one shape for every
        // row, so a flag that answered "the canvas is translucent" would
        // say `alpha: true` on an H.264, a GIF and a WAV — three files with
        // nowhere to put it. It is `audio`'s kind of fact, one line up, and
        // told the same way: what the file can HOLD, never what this
        // document happens to be. A transparent canvas written through a
        // row that answers FALSE composites onto black, and this is the key
        // that lets the sheet and the `export` tool say so before the file
        // exists.
        out.insert("alpha".to_string(), Value::from(preset.alpha));
        out.insert("optimize_for_network".to_string(), Value::from(optimize));
        // An HLG project's video row names the picture it writes (spec §11
        // HD-14 (c)): `hlg`, `pq` or `sdr`; only then, so every SDR project's
        // settings are the text they were and an audio row is unchanged.
        if let Some(picture) = picture {
            out.insert("color".to_string(), Value::from(picture.as_str()));
        }
        // The encoder route on a writer row, and its speed where the encoder
        // has speeds, so every other row's settings are the text they were
        // (spec §11 CX-12, CX-16 (1)).
        if let Some(writer) = preset.writer {
            out.insert("writer".to_string(), Value::from(writer.as_str()));
        }
        if let Some(speed) = speed {
            out.insert("encoder_speed".to_string(), Value::from(speed.as_str()));
        }
        // The depth on the two uncompressed rows only, so every other row's
        // settings are the text they were (spec §11 AU-29).
        if let Some(bits) = bits {
            out.insert("audio_bits".to_string(), Value::from(bits));
        }
        // The stems last, and only when asked, so every other export's
        // settings are the text they were (spec §11 AU-108).
        if let Some(stems) = stems {
            let stems = stems.iter().map(|stem| stem.to_json()).collect();
            out.insert("stems".to_string(), Value::Array(stems));
        }
        Ok(Value::Object(out))
    }
}

//! The containers Frame Job writes (spec §7.8), and the four questions
//! each one answers about ITSELF: its extension, its UTI, whether a
//! leading index is worth rewriting, whether it can hold sound at all.
//!
//! Moved whole out of `export_table.rs` under the size rule (core/CLAUDE.md:
//! a module at ~600 lines gives something up BEFORE it takes anything on),
//! as codec-export C1's toll T1, a pure move. `export_table.rs` re-exports
//! [`Container`], so every `crate::export_table::Container` path holds.

/// The containers Frame Job writes. Kept as an enum rather than a string so
/// the container, its UTI, its network-optimization default and whether it
/// can hold sound at all are decided in exactly one exhaustive place.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub(crate) enum Container {
    Mov,
    Mp4,
    M4a,
    Wav,
    Aiff,
    Gif,
    Apng,
    /// WebM, written by the core's own writer (`webm_writer.rs`, spec §11
    /// CX-15), not by AVFoundation.
    Webm,
}

impl Container {
    pub(crate) fn as_str(self) -> &'static str {
        match self {
            Container::Mov => "mov",
            Container::Mp4 => "mp4",
            Container::M4a => "m4a",
            Container::Wav => "wav",
            Container::Aiff => "aiff",
            Container::Gif => "gif",
            Container::Apng => "apng",
            Container::Webm => "webm",
        }
    }

    /// The output file's UTI. On the AVFoundation path Swift passes this
    /// straight to `outputFileType`, whose raw values ARE UTIs; on the
    /// ImageIO path it is the destination type
    /// `CGImageDestinationCreateWithURL` takes. Either way it is what the
    /// save panel's content type and file extension are derived from, which
    /// is why the two image formats name a real UTI here rather than
    /// nothing — an export that could not tell the panel it writes a `.gif`
    /// would be worse off than one whose type string a session would
    /// reject, and a session is never built for them.
    ///
    /// An APNG is written to an ordinary PNG destination — the animation
    /// lives in `acTL`/`fcTL` chunks inside a file that is still a PNG — so
    /// its type identifier is PNG's, not one of its own.
    ///
    /// The two LPCM rows' identifiers are exactly `AVFileType.wav` and
    /// `AVFileType.aiff`'s raw values, which is what lets
    /// `Exporter.fileType(for:)` resolve them from this string alone
    /// rather than growing a container list of its own.
    pub(crate) fn file_type(self) -> &'static str {
        match self {
            Container::Mov => "com.apple.quicktime-movie",
            Container::Mp4 => "public.mpeg-4",
            Container::M4a => "com.apple.m4a-audio",
            Container::Wav => "com.microsoft.waveform-audio",
            Container::Aiff => "public.aiff-audio",
            Container::Gif => "com.compuserve.gif",
            Container::Apng => "public.png",
            Container::Webm => "org.webmproject.webm",
        }
    }

    /// Whether `shouldOptimizeForNetworkUse` defaults on. MP4 and M4A are
    /// the delivery containers people upload, and a leading `moov` atom
    /// lets a player start before the file is fully fetched. A ProRes MOV
    /// is an editing master handed to another application, where the
    /// rewrite pass buys nothing. An HEVC-with-alpha MOV is a delivery file
    /// too, and its default stays off all the same: that was the owner's
    /// choice for the row (spec §11 G-33), and the override turns it on. GIF
    /// and APNG have no `moov` atom and nothing to move: a viewer already
    /// starts on the first frame, and
    /// neither do WAV and AIFF — a RIFF or an IFF file is a header
    /// followed by samples, with no index to relocate, so the flag would
    /// be a request the writer has nothing to do about. A WebM's index (its
    /// SeekHead) is already at the front, so the flag is off and inert there
    /// too (spec §11 CX-15 (1)).
    pub(crate) fn optimizes_for_network(self) -> bool {
        matches!(self, Container::Mp4 | Container::M4a)
    }

    /// Whether the container can hold sound. Every movie container can,
    /// and M4A, WAV and AIFF are nothing else; GIF and APNG are picture
    /// formats with no audio track in the file format at all, so
    /// `audio: false` for those is a fact about the container rather than
    /// a decision about the project.
    pub(crate) fn carries_audio(self) -> bool {
        !matches!(self, Container::Gif | Container::Apng)
    }
}
//! The bundled encoders a row's video can take (spec §11 CX-12): the route,
//! [`Writer`], and the speed vocabulary, [`EncoderSpeed`].
//!
//! Moved whole out of `export_table.rs` under the size rule (core/CLAUDE.md:
//! a module at ~600 lines gives something up BEFORE it takes anything on),
//! as codec-export C3's toll T1, a pure move. `export_table.rs` re-exports
//! both, so every `crate::export_table::Writer` and
//! `crate::export_table::EncoderSpeed` path holds.

/// The route a row's video takes when it is not AVFoundation's own
/// encoder (spec §11 CX-12 (1)): `None` on a row means VideoToolbox or
/// ProRes through `AVAssetWriter`, or ImageIO where `avf_preset` is null.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub(crate) enum Writer {
    /// SVT-AV1, bundled and called from Swift (CX-2, CX-4).
    SvtAv1,
    /// libvpx's VP9, bundled and called from Swift (CX-3, CX-4, CX-16).
    Libvpx,
}

impl Writer {
    pub(crate) fn as_str(self) -> &'static str {
        match self {
            Writer::SvtAv1 => "svt_av1",
            Writer::Libvpx => "libvpx",
        }
    }

    /// Whether the encoder has speeds to choose from: SVT-AV1's three
    /// (CX-2), and not libvpx's one (CX-3, CX-16 (2)).
    pub(crate) fn takes_speed(self) -> bool {
        match self {
            Writer::SvtAv1 => true,
            Writer::Libvpx => false,
        }
    }
}

/// A bundled encoder's speed (spec §11 CX-2, CX-12 (2)): Faster, Balanced
/// and Smaller file are SVT-AV1's presets 8, 6 and 4 — the number is the
/// app's to map, the word is the vocabulary the sheet and `export` share.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub(crate) enum EncoderSpeed {
    Faster,
    Balanced,
    Smaller,
}

impl EncoderSpeed {
    /// Every speed, in the sheet's order.
    pub(crate) const ALL: [EncoderSpeed; 3] = [
        EncoderSpeed::Faster,
        EncoderSpeed::Balanced,
        EncoderSpeed::Smaller,
    ];

    pub(crate) fn parse(text: &str) -> Option<Self> {
        Self::ALL.into_iter().find(|speed| speed.as_str() == text)
    }

    pub(crate) fn as_str(self) -> &'static str {
        match self {
            EncoderSpeed::Faster => "faster",
            EncoderSpeed::Balanced => "balanced",
            EncoderSpeed::Smaller => "smaller",
        }
    }
}
//! The stems an audio export writes beside its mix (spec §11 AU-108): which
//! timeline tracks each file holds and what it is called. Swift writes them
//! (`ExportStems.swift`); this answers only the grouping and the names, so
//! the sheet, the `export` tool and the tests read one list.
//!
//! The rules:
//!
//! - **Heard.** A timeline track is heard when the plan has an audio track
//!   labelled with it (`CompTrack::track`, AU-107 — so a track holding only
//!   pictures, or nothing, is not) and it is not `silenced` among the
//!   timeline's tracks (mute, solo). None heard is refused.
//! - **Grouping.** `tracks`: one stem per heard track. `names`: heard tracks
//!   whose trimmed names match case-insensitively are one stem, named by the
//!   first's spelling; an unnamed track is its own.
//! - **Order.** Timeline order of each stem's first track; a stem's tracks
//!   in timeline order.
//! - **Names.** The trimmed track name, else `Track N` (N = its index + 1,
//!   the header's label), with `/` and `:` replaced by `-`; a name equal
//!   case-insensitively to an earlier stem's gets ` (Track N)` of its first
//!   track, and one still equal to an earlier stem's then gets
//!   ` (Track N, 2)`, ` (Track N, 3)` and on, the first no earlier stem
//!   holds, so no two stems share a file.

use std::collections::BTreeSet;

use serde_json::{Map, Value};

use crate::audio::silenced;
use crate::export_overrides::StemsMode;
use crate::model::Project;

/// One stem: the file's name (without the mix's) and the timeline tracks
/// whose sound it holds.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub(crate) struct Stem {
    pub(crate) name: String,
    pub(crate) tracks: Vec<u32>,
}

impl Stem {
    /// `{"name": …, "tracks": [ids]}`, the settings' `stems` entry.
    pub(crate) fn to_json(&self) -> Value {
        let mut out = Map::new();
        out.insert("name".to_string(), Value::from(self.name.clone()));
        let tracks = self.tracks.iter().map(|id| Value::from(*id)).collect();
        out.insert("tracks".to_string(), Value::Array(tracks));
        Value::Object(out)
    }
}

/// A group while it is being gathered: its first track's index and the
/// spelling it is named by.
struct Group {
    first: usize,
    spelling: String,
    tracks: Vec<u32>,
}

impl Project {
    /// The stems `mode` asks for, in order (spec §11 AU-108 (b), (c)), or
    /// the refusal when no track is heard.
    pub(crate) fn export_stems(&self, mode: StemsMode) -> Result<Vec<Stem>, String> {
        let labelled: BTreeSet<u32> = self.assign().audio.iter().map(|t| t.track).collect();
        let mut groups: Vec<Group> = Vec::new();
        for (index, track) in self.tracks.iter().enumerate() {
            if !labelled.contains(&track.id) || silenced(&self.tracks, track) {
                continue;
            }
            let trimmed = track.name.trim();
            let joins = match mode {
                StemsMode::Tracks => None,
                StemsMode::Names if trimmed.is_empty() => None,
                StemsMode::Names => groups
                    .iter_mut()
                    .find(|g| !g.spelling.is_empty() && same(&g.spelling, trimmed)),
            };
            match joins {
                Some(group) => group.tracks.push(track.id),
                None => groups.push(Group {
                    first: index,
                    spelling: trimmed.to_string(),
                    tracks: vec![track.id],
                }),
            }
        }
        if groups.is_empty() {
            return Err("stems: no track is heard, so there is no stem to write".to_string());
        }
        let mut stems: Vec<Stem> = Vec::with_capacity(groups.len());
        for group in groups {
            let label = format!("Track {}", group.first.saturating_add(1));
            let base = if group.spelling.is_empty() {
                label.clone()
            } else {
                group.spelling.replace(['/', ':'], "-")
            };
            let taken = |name: &str| stems.iter().any(|s| same(&s.name, name));
            let mut name = base.clone();
            if taken(&name) {
                name = format!("{base} ({label})");
            }
            let mut count: u32 = 2;
            while taken(&name) {
                name = format!("{base} ({label}, {count})");
                count = count.saturating_add(1);
            }
            stems.push(Stem {
                name,
                tracks: group.tracks,
            });
        }
        Ok(stems)
    }
}

/// Two names the same but for case.
fn same(a: &str, b: &str) -> bool {
    a.to_lowercase() == b.to_lowercase()
}
//! How an export override is READ and REFUSED — the vocabulary the export
//! settings resolve from, where `export_table.rs` is the table and
//! `export.rs` the resolution.
//!
//! Cut out of `export.rs` in road map 2's phase 5, and cut HERE for the
//! reason `props_check.rs` was cut out of `edit_props.rs`: a rule about
//! how a value is spelled and what makes it a refusal is a fact about the
//! VOCABULARY, not about the resolution. Nothing in this file knows which
//! preset asked, what the canvas is, or what the settings object ends up
//! looking like; everything in it answers one question — "is this JSON
//! value a legal `width` / `fps` / `range` / bitrate / decibel figure, and
//! if not, what do we tell the caller it was?".
//!
//! The cut also paid the size rule's toll. `export.rs` stood at 578 lines
//! against the ~600 soft cap and phase 5 added two containers, two preset
//! rows, a decibel validator, two override reads and two echo lines; a
//! file at the line gives something up before it takes anything on.
//!
//! Three rules hold for every reader here, and every new one must keep
//! them, because the sheet, the `export` tool and the test suite all lean
//! on them:
//!
//! 1. **Absent and explicit null are the same thing** — "use the preset".
//!    That is what lets the export sheet build one overrides object and
//!    null the fields whose control is greyed out.
//! 2. **An unknown key is ignored, silently.** A newer UI may send fields
//!    this build has never heard of and must still get an export.
//! 3. **A refusal names the offending value**, through [`describe`], and
//!    the message is lowercase and starts `export <key> must be …`. The
//!    key is in the message because a caller sending six overrides needs
//!    to know which one it was.
//!
//! [`describe`] itself is not about export at all any more: five modules
//! outside this one (`edit.rs`, `edit_props.rs`, `edit_action.rs`,
//! `edit_paste.rs`, `edit_track.rs`) name it `crate::export::describe` for
//! their own error messages, so `export.rs` re-exports it under that path
//! and the cut costs those files nothing. [`spelled`] sits beside it for
//! the same reason: a vocabulary value's own JSON spelling, for a sentence
//! (`lut.rs`, `media_color.rs`, `edit_relink.rs`).

use serde_json::Value;

use crate::dynamic_range::DynamicRange;
use crate::export_table::EncoderSpeed;
use crate::time::{Fps, Ticks};

// ---- the shared front door -----------------------------------------------

/// A JSON value as short text for an error message, truncated on a char
/// boundary so a pasted megabyte of JSON cannot become the error string.
pub(crate) fn describe(value: &Value) -> String {
    let text = value.to_string();
    if text.chars().count() <= 40 {
        text
    } else {
        format!("{} …", text.chars().take(40).collect::<String>())
    }
}

/// A vocabulary value's JSON spelling (`warm`, `video`), serde's own, so a
/// sentence names it as the file and the schema do.
pub(crate) fn spelled<T: serde::Serialize>(value: T) -> String {
    serde_json::to_value(value)
        .ok()
        .and_then(|value| value.as_str().map(str::to_string))
        .unwrap_or_default()
}

/// The overrides object itself: `None` for absent, null or an empty
/// request, `Some` for an object, and an `Err` naming the value for
/// anything else. One place, so "overrides must be a json object" is the
/// same sentence however the caller got here.
pub(crate) fn object(overrides: Option<&Value>) -> Result<Option<&Value>, String> {
    match overrides {
        None => Ok(None),
        Some(Value::Null) => Ok(None),
        Some(value) if value.is_object() => Ok(Some(value)),
        Some(value) => Err(format!(
            "export overrides must be a json object, not {}",
            describe(value)
        )),
    }
}

/// An override field, or `None` when absent or explicitly null (null means
/// "use the preset", so a caller can build one overrides object and leave
/// the fields it does not care about).
fn field<'a>(overrides: Option<&'a Value>, key: &str) -> Option<&'a Value> {
    overrides.and_then(|o| o.get(key)).filter(|v| !v.is_null())
}

// ---- numbers -------------------------------------------------------------

/// A strictly positive integer override — the pixel dimensions and the two
/// bitrates, which want the identical check. `u64::MAX` is not the ceiling
/// on purpose: a bitrate is bits per second, and `u32`'s 4.3 Gbit/s is an
/// order of magnitude past anything deliverable (ProRes 422 at 4K is about
/// 0.7), so the same bound that keeps a width sane keeps a bitrate honest.
/// Zero and negative both land in the error arm, which names the value.
pub(crate) fn positive_u32(overrides: Option<&Value>, key: &str) -> Result<Option<u32>, String> {
    let Some(value) = field(overrides, key) else {
        return Ok(None);
    };
    match value.as_u64() {
        Some(n) if n > 0 && n <= u64::from(u32::MAX) => Ok(Some(n as u32)),
        _ => Err(format!(
            "export {key} must be a positive integer, not {}",
            describe(value)
        )),
    }
}

/// A decibel override: a finite number inside `[min, max]`. The f64 twin
/// of [`positive_u32`], and a separate function because the two loudness
/// figures are NEGATIVE FRACTIONS — −14.5 LUFS is a real request — so
/// every guard `positive_u32` applies is the wrong one here.
///
/// `as_f64` is the whole type check: `serde_json` answers `None` for a
/// bool, a string, an array and an object alike, so the error arm catches
/// them all and names what arrived. Non-finite is checked even though JSON
/// cannot spell a NaN or an infinity — the function takes a `Value` from
/// whoever has one, and a bound comparison against a NaN is silently false
/// rather than an error.
fn decibels(
    overrides: Option<&Value>,
    key: &str,
    min: f64,
    max: f64,
) -> Result<Option<f64>, String> {
    let Some(value) = field(overrides, key) else {
        return Ok(None);
    };
    match value.as_f64() {
        Some(db) if db.is_finite() && db >= min && db <= max => Ok(Some(db)),
        _ => Err(format!(
            "export {key} must be a number from {min} to {max}, not {}",
            describe(value)
        )),
    }
}

/// The bottom of the loudness target's range. −70 LUFS is BS.1770-4's own
/// absolute gate: a programme quieter than that measures as nothing at
/// all, so a target at or below it is a request the measurement cannot
/// even express. The top is 0 LUFS — full scale, unwise but legal, and the
/// bound is where it is so the message is about the value.
const LOUDNESS_MIN: f64 = -70.0;
const LOUDNESS_MAX: f64 = 0.0;

/// The true-peak ceiling's range. 0 dBTP is legal (and what a master with
/// no headroom asks for); −20 is far below anything a deliverable wants
/// and exists only so a mistyped LUFS figure lands in the error arm rather
/// than turning the programme down by 60 dB.
const PEAK_MIN: f64 = -20.0;
const PEAK_MAX: f64 = 0.0;

/// The two loudness overrides, read together: `loudness_lufs`, the
/// integrated target the export is normalised to, and `peak_dbtp`, the
/// true-peak ceiling of the export's master limiter.
///
/// **Either may come alone** (spec §11 AU-27). Every export with sound runs
/// through a master limiter at `peak_dbtp`, −1 dBTP when it is not asked
/// for, so a ceiling on its own is not inert: it is the limiter's ceiling,
/// with or without a target, and is accepted and echoed as every other
/// override is.
///
/// Both are emitted by the resolver only when asked for, exactly as the
/// bitrates are: an absent `loudness_lufs` is "do not normalise", which is
/// what `ExportSettings.loudnessLufs == nil` says on the Swift side, and an
/// absent `peak_dbtp` is the master's default ceiling there.
pub(crate) fn loudness(overrides: Option<&Value>) -> Result<(Option<f64>, Option<f64>), String> {
    let target = decibels(overrides, "loudness_lufs", LOUDNESS_MIN, LOUDNESS_MAX)?;
    let ceiling = decibels(overrides, "peak_dbtp", PEAK_MIN, PEAK_MAX)?;
    Ok((target, ceiling))
}

/// The `audio_bits` override (spec §11 AU-29): 16 or 24, `None` when absent
/// or null. Whether the row takes one is the resolver's question
/// (`Preset::takes_audio_bits`); this answers only whether the value is one
/// of the two depths, so a 20 or a string is refused by name on any row.
pub(crate) fn audio_bits(overrides: Option<&Value>) -> Result<Option<u32>, String> {
    let Some(value) = field(overrides, "audio_bits") else {
        return Ok(None);
    };
    match value.as_u64() {
        Some(bits @ (16 | 24)) => Ok(Some(bits as u32)),
        _ => Err(format!(
            "export audio_bits must be 16 or 24, not {}",
            describe(value)
        )),
    }
}

/// True or false, or `default` when the key is absent — the shape every
/// flag override wants and only `optimize_for_network` has so far.
pub(crate) fn boolean(overrides: Option<&Value>, key: &str, default: bool) -> Result<bool, String> {
    match field(overrides, key) {
        None => Ok(default),
        Some(value) => value.as_bool().ok_or_else(|| {
            format!(
                "export {key} must be true or false, not {}",
                describe(value)
            )
        }),
    }
}

/// The `dynamic_range` override (spec §11 HD-14 (c)): `hlg`, `pq` or `sdr`,
/// `None` when absent or null. Which of them a row may write is the
/// resolver's question (`Project::deliverable`); this answers only whether
/// the value is one of the three words.
pub(crate) fn dynamic_range(overrides: Option<&Value>) -> Result<Option<DynamicRange>, String> {
    let Some(value) = field(overrides, "dynamic_range") else {
        return Ok(None);
    };
    match value.as_str().and_then(DynamicRange::parse) {
        Some(range) => Ok(Some(range)),
        None => Err(format!(
            "export dynamic_range must be \"hlg\", \"pq\" or \"sdr\", not {}",
            describe(value)
        )),
    }
}

/// The `encoder_speed` override (spec §11 CX-12 (2)): `faster`, `balanced`
/// or `smaller`, `None` when absent or null. Whether the row takes one is
/// the resolver's question; this answers only whether it is one of the
/// three words, so a misspelling is refused by name on any row.
pub(crate) fn encoder_speed(overrides: Option<&Value>) -> Result<Option<EncoderSpeed>, String> {
    let Some(value) = field(overrides, "encoder_speed") else {
        return Ok(None);
    };
    match value.as_str().and_then(EncoderSpeed::parse) {
        Some(speed) => Ok(Some(speed)),
        None => Err(format!(
            "export encoder_speed must be \"faster\", \"balanced\" or \"smaller\", not {}",
            describe(value)
        )),
    }
}

/// How an audio export groups its stems (spec §11 AU-108 (b)): one per
/// heard timeline track, or one per track name.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub(crate) enum StemsMode {
    Tracks,
    Names,
}

/// The `stems` override (spec §11 AU-108): `tracks` or `names`, `None` when
/// absent or null. Whether the row takes one is the resolver's question
/// (`Preset::takes_stems`); this answers only whether it is one of the two
/// words, so a misspelling is refused by name on any row.
pub(crate) fn stems(overrides: Option<&Value>) -> Result<Option<StemsMode>, String> {
    let Some(value) = field(overrides, "stems") else {
        return Ok(None);
    };
    match value.as_str() {
        Some("tracks") => Ok(Some(StemsMode::Tracks)),
        Some("names") => Ok(Some(StemsMode::Names)),
        _ => Err(format!(
            "export stems must be \"tracks\" or \"names\", not {}",
            describe(value)
        )),
    }
}

// ---- compound values -----------------------------------------------------

/// The `fps` override as `[num, den]`, or `default` when it is absent.
/// Both terms are read as `u64` and bounded by `u32::MAX` because that is
/// what [`Fps`] holds, and both must be non-zero: a zero numerator is a
/// frozen export and a zero denominator is a division by nothing, and
/// either would reach the frame-snapping arithmetic before anyone noticed.
pub(crate) fn fps(overrides: Option<&Value>, default: Fps) -> Result<Fps, String> {
    let Some(value) = field(overrides, "fps") else {
        return Ok(default);
    };
    let terms = value.as_array().and_then(|a| {
        match (
            a.first().and_then(Value::as_u64),
            a.get(1).and_then(Value::as_u64),
        ) {
            (Some(num), Some(den)) if a.len() == 2 => Some((num, den)),
            _ => None,
        }
    });
    match terms {
        Some((num, den))
            if num > 0 && den > 0 && num <= u64::from(u32::MAX) && den <= u64::from(u32::MAX) =>
        {
            Ok(Fps {
                num: num as u32,
                den: den as u32,
            })
        }
        _ => Err(format!(
            "export fps must be [num, den] with non-zero terms, not {}",
            describe(value)
        )),
    }
}

/// One end of a range, in ticks.
fn ticks(value: &Value, what: &str) -> Result<Ticks, String> {
    value.as_i64().ok_or_else(|| {
        format!(
            "export {what} must be an integer number of ticks, not {}",
            describe(value)
        )
    })
}

/// The `range` override as the raw `(start, duration)` pair it asked for,
/// each falling back to its half of `default` — which is `(0, the whole
/// project)`, so `{"start": …}` alone keeps the project's length and
/// `{"duration": …}` alone keeps its start.
///
/// RAW is the word: nothing here snaps or clamps. Both are decisions about
/// a project and an export rate, which this file knows nothing about, and
/// the ORDER of the two (snap first, then clamp) is load-bearing enough to
/// stay visible where the settings are resolved.
pub(crate) fn range(
    overrides: Option<&Value>,
    default: (Ticks, Ticks),
) -> Result<(Ticks, Ticks), String> {
    let Some(range) = field(overrides, "range") else {
        return Ok(default);
    };
    if !range.is_object() {
        return Err(format!(
            "export range must be a json object with start and duration, not {}",
            describe(range)
        ));
    }
    let (mut start, mut duration) = default;
    if let Some(value) = field(Some(range), "start") {
        start = ticks(value, "range start")?;
    }
    if let Some(value) = field(Some(range), "duration") {
        duration = ticks(value, "range duration")?;
    }
    Ok((start, duration))
}

    /// **It is a `Preset` field and not a [`Container`] question**, which
    /// is the one thing worth arguing here, because the neighbouring
    /// capability [`Container::carries_audio`] IS a container question and
    /// the two look alike. Whether a file can hold a sound track is decided
    /// by the container's format — a GIF has no audio track to put samples
    /// in whatever you encode — so it is answered once for the container.
    /// A fourth channel is decided by the CODEC: `Container::Mov` hosts
    /// `prores_422` and `prores_4444` alike, and 422 cannot carry alpha at
    /// any bitrate, so a container-level answer would have to be wrong for
    /// one of the two rows. Same shape of fact, different owner.
    ///
    /// **`gif` is false and `apng` is true, and the difference is the word
    /// CHANNEL.** Both can show a transparent pixel, so both look alike
    /// from the outside and a reader who has exported one will call this
    /// row a mistake. APNG is true colour with a real fourth channel and a
    /// pixel may be any fraction of opaque. A GIF has ONE palette index
    /// nominated as transparent: a pixel is either that index or fully
    /// opaque, there is no partial value to store, and a soft edge against
    /// nothing comes out as a hard jagged one. A flag that answered "yes"
    /// for both would license the sheet to promise a translucent GIF.
    /// Measured on the way in, not assumed: an `apng` export of a
    /// transparent canvas decodes `pix_fmt=rgba` with its corner at
    /// alpha 0, and a `gif` of the same timeline decodes with the corner
    /// transparent through its palette and no partial value anywhere.
    pub(crate) alpha: bool,
    /// `None` means "the project's canvas size", which is what every movie
    /// row wants: a preset is an encoding, and the size is the document's
    /// unless a `width`/`height` override says otherwise. The resolver
    /// fills the canvas in for the audio-only rows too, so every settings
    /// object carries a width. Only the two image rows pin one — the cap
    /// `Project::export_sizes` lists as their default.
    pub(crate) size: Option<(u32, u32)>,
    /// `None` means "the project's canvas rate", which is what every movie
    /// preset wants. Only the image-sequence rows pin one, because their
    /// cost is frames × pixels and nothing else.
    pub(crate) fps: Option<Fps>,
    /// The `AVAssetExportSession` preset name, verbatim; `None` for a
    /// container AVFoundation cannot write.
    ///
    /// Since phase 5 the field means only **"AVFoundation can write this
    /// row"**, and the name itself is a record rather than an instruction.
    /// Nothing has run a session since M2, but `null` here is still the
    /// one thing routing an export to the ImageIO writer
    /// (`ExportSettings.isImageSequence` is literally `avPreset == nil`),
    /// so a row that AVFoundation writes must carry SOME name even when
    /// AVFoundation has no name for it. There is no `AVAssetExportPreset`
    /// for LPCM in a WAV or an AIFF, so those two rows carry the audio-only
    /// name `audio_m4a` carries: it is the closest true statement
    /// available — "an audio-only file AVFoundation writes" — and a null
    /// there would hand a WAV to `ImageSequenceWriter`, which would refuse
    /// it as an unknown image container and report a format this table
    /// advertises as unsupported. A name also means "the composition is
    /// read back through AVFoundation" (spec §11 CX-15), which is all it says
    /// on `av1_webm` and `vp9_webm`, whose files the core writes.
    pub(crate) avf_preset: Option<&'static str>,
    /// The bundled encoder that writes the row's video, or `None` for
    /// AVFoundation's own encoders (ImageIO where `avf_preset` is null).
    /// A row with a writer takes `encoder_speed` when its encoder has
    /// speeds (`Writer::takes_speed`).
    pub(crate) writer: Option<Writer>,
}

/// A rate as the two-element `[num, den]` array the spec spells every rate
/// with, or null for "the project's own". One function so the preset row
/// and the resolved settings cannot disagree about the shape.
pub(crate) fn fps_json(fps: Option<Fps>) -> Value {
    match fps {
        Some(fps) => Value::Array(vec![Value::from(fps.num), Value::from(fps.den)]),
        None => Value::Null,
    }
}

impl Preset {
    /// True for the rows written frame by frame through ImageIO — the ones
    /// with no session preset — whose picture is FITTED inside the size
    /// rather than letterboxed to it, so a size on them is a box and the
    /// resolved numbers are the fit (`export_size::fit_within`).
    pub(crate) fn fits_inside_size(&self) -> bool {
        self.video && self.avf_preset.is_none()
    }

    /// True for the rows whose frame has to have EVEN pixel dimensions:
    /// every row AVFoundation writes but ProRes 4444 (spec §11 G-33). On
    /// these an odd edge is resolved one pixel smaller, in the size list's
    /// Document row and in the resolved settings alike
    /// (`export_size::even_size`), so the size reported is the size the
    /// writer writes.
    ///
    /// It is asked of `avf_preset` and the codec rather than listed row by
    /// row, so a new AVFoundation row is even-sized until someone exempts
    /// it — the safe side, since 4:2:0 chroma needs it and a row that can
    /// keep an odd edge has to be measured doing so, as ProRes 4444 was
    /// (`ExporterWriter+Settings.swift`'s `encodedSize`). The audio-only
    /// rows answer true: they have no picture and go on reporting what
    /// `h264` reports. The two image rows answer false; their numbers are
    /// the fit (`fits_inside_size`), odd or not.
    pub(crate) fn even_sized(&self) -> bool {
        self.avf_preset.is_some() && self.codec != "prores4444"
    }

    /// True for a row whose bundled encoder has speeds, the rows
    /// `encoder_speed` applies to (spec §11 CX-12 (2), CX-16 (2)).
    pub(crate) fn takes_encoder_speed(&self) -> bool {
        self.writer.is_some_and(Writer::takes_speed)
    }

    /// True for the uncompressed audio rows, `audio_wav` and `audio_aiff`,
    /// the rows `audio_bits` applies to (spec §11 AU-29). ProRes's LPCM
    /// track keeps 16 bits; it is not an `lpcm` row.
    pub(crate) fn takes_audio_bits(&self) -> bool {
        self.codec == "lpcm"
    }

    /// True for the three audio-only rows, `audio_m4a`, `audio_wav` and
    /// `audio_aiff`, the rows `stems` applies to (spec §11 AU-108 (a)).
    pub(crate) fn takes_stems(&self) -> bool {
        !self.video
    }

    pub(crate) fn to_json(&self) -> Value {
        let mut out = Map::new();
        out.insert("id".to_string(), Value::from(self.id));
        out.insert("name".to_string(), Value::from(self.name));
        out.insert(
            "container".to_string(),
            Value::from(self.container.as_str()),
        );
        out.insert("codec".to_string(), Value::from(self.codec));
        out.insert("video".to_string(), Value::from(self.video));
        out.insert(
            "size".to_string(),
            match self.size {
                Some((w, h)) => Value::Array(vec![Value::from(w), Value::from(h)]),
                None => Value::Null,
            },
        );
        // `size` and `fps` are spelled the same way for the same reason:
        // null is "whatever the project is" — the document size, the
        // document rate — which the resolver fills in from the canvas.
        out.insert("fps".to_string(), fps_json(self.fps));
        out.insert(
            "avf_preset".to_string(),
            match self.avf_preset {
                Some(name) => Value::from(name),
                None => Value::Null,
            },
        );
        if crate::dynamic_range::HDR_ROWS.contains(&self.id) {
            out.insert("hdr".to_string(), Value::from(true));
        }
        if let Some(writer) = self.writer {
            out.insert("writer".to_string(), Value::from(writer.as_str()));
            if writer.takes_speed() {
                let speeds = EncoderSpeed::ALL.iter().map(|s| Value::from(s.as_str()));
                out.insert("encoder_speeds".to_string(), speeds.collect());
            }
        }
        if self.takes_audio_bits() {
            let bits = [16, 24].iter().map(|b| Value::from(*b));
            out.insert("audio_bits".to_string(), bits.collect());
        }
        if self.takes_stems() {
            let modes = ["tracks", "names"].iter().map(|m| Value::from(*m));
            out.insert("stems".to_string(), modes.collect());
        }
        Value::Object(out)
    }
}
//! The output sizes the export sheet offers for one document and one
//! format — `rc_export_sizes` (spec §7.8) — and the one rule for what a
//! size MEANS on an image format, which `export_settings` applies too.
//!
//! A preset is an encoding and carries no size (`export.rs`), so the size
//! is a second choice, and this module is the list that choice is made
//! from: the format's cap if it has one, the document's own size, and the
//! standard sizes fitted to the document's aspect. The FIRST row is the
//! default — the rule every list the export sheet is built from follows —
//! which is the document's size for a movie format and the cap for an
//! image format, because an image format's cap is the point of the row
//! and not a ceiling that only applies from above.
//!
//! **The number is the short edge.** A standard size is named by one
//! number, and the number people use is the short one: "1080p ultrawide"
//! is 2560 × 1080 and "1080p vertical" is 1080 × 1920, and both are 1080p
//! to everyone who says it. Naming by the long edge would call the
//! ultrawide 2560p and the vertical 1920p, which nobody does. So a row
//! keeps its short edge exactly and the long edge follows the document:
//! L = S × long / short of the document, on whichever axis the document is
//! longer along, and S × S for a square.
//!
//! **The long edge is rounded to even.** 4:2:0 chroma puts one colour
//! sample under each 2 × 2 block of luma, so H.264 and HEVC want even
//! dimensions, and `ExporterWriter.evenDimension` rounds an odd one down
//! before writing. Rounding here — to the nearest even integer, a tie
//! going up — means the size the popup shows is the size the file has;
//! leaving it to the writer would change the size behind the sheet's
//! back.
//!
//! **The even rule (spec §11 G-33).** The same is true of the document row
//! and of any size asked for, so on every row that needs even dimensions
//! (`Preset::even_sized`: every row AVFoundation writes but ProRes 4444)
//! the size is resolved HERE, by [`even_size`]: the document row's numbers
//! in this list, and in `export_settings` whatever was asked. An odd edge
//! is one pixel SMALLER. Down, because that is the size the writer already
//! wrote, so the report moves to the file and no file changes size;
//! because rounding up would hand the encoder a frame larger than the
//! picture on that axis; and because every listed row is then its own
//! fixed point, as an image format's is. The row is named "Document
//! (even)" when its numbers are not the canvas's, which is the whole of
//! how the popup says why. The argument this replaces was that a "Document
//! size" that was not the document's size would be a lie; the lie was the
//! row that said 321 × 181 over a file of 320 × 180. ProRes 4444 and the
//! two image formats keep an odd edge, and their row is "Document size",
//! the canvas as it is.
//!
//! **An image format's numbers are the file's.** A GIF or an APNG is
//! written frame by frame with the picture FITTED inside the size — aspect
//! kept, never upscaled — where a movie is letterboxed to exactly the frame
//! asked for (`ExporterImageSequence`, spec §7.8): padding costs bytes in
//! every frame for pixels that carry nothing, and a bigger frame carries no
//! more detail. So on those two formats every row here, and every size
//! `export_settings` resolves — the cap, a standard row, a custom size — is
//! the canvas fitted inside that box ([`fit_within`]), so that the popup,
//! the sheet's readout and the `export` tool all report the numbers the
//! file will have: a vertical 1080 × 1920 timeline's "GIF default" is
//! 152 × 270, not 480 × 270. The fit is decided here, once; the writer
//! draws each frame at exactly this size and does not fit a second time.
//! It is also its own fixed point — a fitted size fed back as the box
//! fits to itself — because that is exactly what happens: the sheet and
//! the tool send a listed row's numbers back through `export_settings` as
//! `width` and `height`, and a fit that could move a derived edge by a
//! pixel on the second pass would show one size and write another.
//!
//! **Rows may repeat.** A 1920 × 1080 document's 1080p row IS the document
//! row, and on a 1080 × 1920 document every image-format row from 1080p up
//! is the document again. They are listed anyway, because an id is the
//! `export` tool's vocabulary and a request for `1080p` has to mean the
//! same thing on every document — refusing it exactly where it is a no-op
//! would be the popup's tidiness leaking into an API. The sheet hides a
//! row whose numbers an earlier row already has, so a person never sees one
//! size under two names, and the row that shows is the one whose name says
//! more ("Document size" over "1080p"). Rows larger than the document are
//! offered for a movie — they were before, as the 4K preset — because a
//! 720p screen recording exported at 4K for a platform that picks its
//! bitrate by resolution is a real request.

use serde_json::{Map, Value};

use crate::export;
use crate::model::Project;

/// The standard sizes, largest first, as (id, name, short edge). The id is
/// the `export` tool's vocabulary and the name is the popup's; they differ
/// only for 4K, which nobody calls 2160p out loud but which needs a
/// short-edge id to sit in the same vocabulary as the other three.
const STANDARD: [(&str, &str, u32); 4] = [
    ("2160p", "4K", 2160),
    ("1440p", "1440p", 1440),
    ("1080p", "1080p", 1080),
    ("720p", "720p", 720),
];

/// One row of the list, before it is JSON.
struct Row {
    id: &'static str,
    name: String,
    width: u32,
    height: u32,
}

impl Row {
    fn to_json(&self) -> Value {
        let mut out = Map::new();
        out.insert("id".to_string(), Value::from(self.id));
        out.insert("name".to_string(), Value::from(self.name.as_str()));
        out.insert("width".to_string(), Value::from(self.width));
        out.insert("height".to_string(), Value::from(self.height));
        Value::Object(out)
    }
}

impl Project {
    /// The sizes the export sheet offers for `preset_id` against this
    /// document, as the JSON array of `{id, name, width, height}` rows
    /// `rc_export_sizes` returns, in menu order — the first is the default.
    ///
    /// The rows:
    ///
    /// 1. `preset` — the format's cap, "GIF default" / "APNG default", for
    ///    the two rows of the table that carry one; nothing for the rest.
    /// 2. `document` — "Document size", the canvas as it is; on a row that
    ///    needs even dimensions the canvas evened ([`even_size`]), named
    ///    "Document (even)" when that is not the canvas's own size.
    /// 3. `2160p` ("4K"), `1440p`, `1080p`, `720p` — the short edge named,
    ///    the long edge fitted to the canvas and rounded to even.
    ///
    /// On an image format every row's numbers are the canvas fitted inside
    /// that row's box, never upscaled — the size the file will have. Rows
    /// may repeat an earlier row's numbers and are listed anyway (the
    /// module doc says why); the sheet hides the repeats.
    ///
    /// One list for every movie format, the audio-only row included: a
    /// width is reported for it the way `export_settings` reports one, and
    /// the sheet disables the control rather than this module keeping a
    /// second rule for one row. `Err` for an unknown preset, naming it —
    /// the same lookup and the same words as `export_settings`.
    pub fn export_sizes(&self, preset_id: &str) -> Result<Value, String> {
        let preset = export::preset(preset_id)?;
        let document = (self.canvas.width, self.canvas.height);
        let fits = preset.fits_inside_size();
        let sized = |(width, height): (u32, u32)| {
            if fits {
                fit_within(document, (width, height))
            } else {
                (width, height)
            }
        };

        let mut rows: Vec<Row> = Vec::new();
        if let Some(cap) = preset.size {
            // The codec in capitals is the format's name as people write
            // it — GIF, APNG — for the two rows that carry a cap.
            let (width, height) = sized(cap);
            rows.push(Row {
                id: "preset",
                name: format!("{} default", preset.codec.to_uppercase()),
                width,
                height,
            });
        }
        // The id is `document` either way: the name is the popup's, and it
        // changes only when the numbers are not the canvas's.
        let listed = if preset.even_sized() {
            even_size(document)
        } else {
            document
        };
        let name = if listed == document {
            "Document size"
        } else {
            "Document (even)"
        };
        rows.push(Row {
            id: "document",
            name: name.to_string(),
            width: listed.0,
            height: listed.1,
        });
        for (id, name, short) in STANDARD {
            let (width, height) = sized(fitted(document, short));
            rows.push(Row {
                id,
                name: name.to_string(),
                width,
                height,
            });
        }

        Ok(Value::Array(rows.iter().map(Row::to_json).collect()))
    }
}

/// The size whose short edge is `short`, in the aspect of a `width` ×
/// `height` document: the long edge is `short` × the document's long edge
/// over its short one, rounded to the nearest even integer with a tie
/// going up, on the axis the document is longer along. A square document
/// gives `short` × `short`.
fn fitted((width, height): (u32, u32), short: u32) -> (u32, u32) {
    let doc_long = u64::from(width.max(height));
    // `validate` refuses a zero edge; the `max` keeps the division total
    // regardless, since no input may panic.
    let doc_short = u64::from(width.min(height)).max(1);

    // The nearest even integer to short × doc_long / doc_short is twice
    // the nearest integer to half of it, and round(a / b) with a tie going
    // up is (2a + b) / 2b in integer division — here a = short × doc_long
    // and b = 2 × doc_short, so the whole thing is one division. u64
    // because a canvas need only be non-zero (`validate`), not inside
    // `set_canvas`'s 16..16384, so the product is not bounded by u32.
    let half = (u64::from(short) * doc_long + doc_short) / (2 * doc_short);
    // A canvas far outside those bounds can carry a ratio no u32 holds;
    // the row is then unusable, but a query must answer, and evenly.
    let long = u32::try_from(half * 2).unwrap_or(u32::MAX - 1);

    if width >= height {
        (long, short)
    } else {
        (short, long)
    }
}

/// A size as a row that needs even dimensions writes it (spec §11 G-33),
/// each edge on its own: below 2 it is 2, an odd edge is one SMALLER, and an
/// even edge is itself. The rule `export_settings` and
/// [`Project::export_sizes`] share, so the list and the resolver cannot
/// disagree about what an H.264 will measure; the module doc says why down.
/// It is `ExporterWriter.evenDimension`'s arithmetic, which stays as the
/// floor under any caller that did not come through here.
pub(crate) fn even_size((width, height): (u32, u32)) -> (u32, u32) {
    let even = |edge: u32| if edge < 2 { 2 } else { edge - edge % 2 };
    (even(width), even(height))
}

/// A `width` × `height` canvas fitted inside a box: the canvas itself when
/// it already fits — never upscaled — and otherwise scaled down, aspect
/// kept, so that the edge which meets the box first takes the box's value
/// exactly and the other is the scaled value rounded UP to a whole pixel.
/// The rule an image format's size means (module doc), shared by
/// `export_settings` and [`Project::export_sizes`] so the two cannot
/// disagree about what a GIF will measure.
///
/// Up rather than to the nearest, so that the fit is its own fixed point.
/// Fitting the result again compares box × canvas products on the two
/// axes, and the derived edge — never below its exact value — keeps the
/// same axis limiting, whose derived edge is the same ceiling; rounded to
/// the nearest it can fall below the exact value, flip the limiting axis
/// on the second pass, and take a pixel off the other edge (a 3024 × 1964
/// recording's 1080p box, 1662 × 1080, came back 1662 × 1079 and then
/// 1661 × 1079). The ceiling never leaves the box: the limiting axis is
/// the one whose exact scaled value fits, and a whole number no smaller
/// than a value that fits inside a whole box edge fits too.
pub(crate) fn fit_within((width, height): (u32, u32), (box_w, box_h): (u32, u32)) -> (u32, u32) {
    if width <= box_w && height <= box_h {
        return (width, height);
    }
    let (w, h) = (u64::from(width).max(1), u64::from(height).max(1));
    let (bw, bh) = (u64::from(box_w).max(1), u64::from(box_h).max(1));
    // The width meets the box first when box_w / w ≤ box_h / h, which is
    // box_w × h ≤ box_h × w with no division to round.
    let (fw, fh) = if bw * h <= bh * w {
        (bw, ceil_div(h * bw, w))
    } else {
        (ceil_div(w * bh, h), bh)
    };
    let edge = |value: u64| u32::try_from(value).unwrap_or(u32::MAX).max(1);
    (edge(fw), edge(fh))
}

/// `a / b` rounded up to a whole number; `b ≥ 1`.
fn ceil_div(a: u64, b: u64) -> u64 {
    a.div_ceil(b)
}

